.TH GPU-SWITCHER 1 "@DATE@" "gpu-switcher @VERSION@" "User Manual"
.SH NAME
gpu-switcher \- switch the primary display adapter (GNOME/Mutter)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B gpu-switcher
.B gpu-switcher list | status | version
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gpu-switcher
is a small GTK 4 GUI application for machines with more than one GPU. It picks
which GPU the GNOME compositor (Mutter) uses as the
.B primary
display adapter by managing the udev rule
.B /etc/udev/rules.d/61-mutter-primary-gpu.rules
for the next session start.
.PP
The radio buttons edit the preference;
.B "In use now"
marks the GPU GNOME is actually using. Each GPU row shows its own display
status. Expand its hardware details to see the full name, PCI address and
device node without selecting it.
.PP
Select a different GPU and press
.B Apply
to write the rule for it, or apply
.B "System default (auto)"
to remove the rule and let Mutter choose again. An unsaved choice does not
change the running adapter or the rule on disk. Changing the preference is
supported only in a GNOME/Wayland session.
.PP
Each rule change is authorized by polkit (action
.BR com.github.wl04.gpu_switcher.set-primary ,
administrator authentication, not cached: a call that succeeded a moment ago is
authorized again). The helper then writes the rule and udev is reloaded and
re-triggered
.RB ( udevadm " control \-\-reload-rules" ,
.BR "udevadm trigger" ,
.BR "udevadm info" ),
to refresh the tags before the next session. For a pending change, the window
offers
.B "Log out"
if a new login is sufficient, or
.B "Reboot now"
if a reboot is needed. Neither button is shown otherwise.
.SH COMMANDS
With no command
.B gpu-switcher
opens the window. With a command it answers in the terminal, and it needs no
display, so it works over SSH as well:
.TP
.B list
One row per display GPU: the
.B /dev/dri
card name, its PCI address, the model name, then the terminal badges:
.BR "In use now" ,
.BR "Preferred (configured)" ,
the outputs that report a monitor, or
.BR "No display connected" .
A card whose kernel data could not be read carries no display badge at all.
.TP
.B status
What the managed rule pins (or that there is none), any other udev rule that
sets the same tag, whether udev currently applies the tag on the configured
card, which adapter the desktop runs on now, and what all of that adds up to -
for example
.IR "The change applies after a reboot."
or
.IR "GNOME is using the configured GPU."
.TP
.B version
Prints the version of the installed package.
.PP
A command exits 0 when it answered, 2 when the command line was wrong (usage on
stderr) and 1 when nothing could be read.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/udev/rules.d/61-mutter-primary-gpu.rules
The managed udev rule. Before a write replaces it, the contents it replaces are
copied to
.BI rule ".bak.\fIUTC-timestamp\fR.\fIrandom-suffix\fR"
(for example
.BR 61-mutter-primary-gpu.rules.bak.20260301T120000Z.a1b2c3 ,
the five newest backups are kept).
.B "apt remove"
leaves the rule and its backups in place: they record the user's choice, not
package data. To restore automatic selection manually, delete the managed rule
and reboot so udev re-evaluates the devices.
.SH HELPER
The rule is written by a root helper: the systemd unit
.B gpu-switcher-helper.service
with the D-Bus name
.BR com.github.wl04.gpu_switcher.Helper .
dbus-daemon starts it on demand and it exits once it has been idle. Its sandbox
permits writes only to
.BR /etc/udev/rules.d ,
so the app itself never holds privileged access. The helper's log is read with
.HP
.B "journalctl \-u gpu-switcher-helper.service"
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
What udev actually applied to a card:
.HP
.B "udevadm info /sys/class/drm/card1 | grep CURRENT_TAGS"
.PP
The tag
.B mutter-device-preferred-primary
on the chosen card is what Mutter reads. If it is missing, the rule is not on
disk, udev was not reloaded, or
.B "udevadm verify"
rejected the rule.
.SH "REPORTING BUGS"
Report problems at
.UR https://github.com/wl04/gpu-switcher
.UE ,
include the installed version
.RB ( "dpkg-query \-W gpu-switcher" ) ,
the GPU list
.RB ( "lspci \-nn" )
and the last lines of
.BR "journalctl \-u gpu-switcher-helper.service" .
.SH AUTHORS
Written by wl04.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR udevadm (8),
.BR lspci (8)
